{
  "name": "F4L Igniting Education's Future in Tanzania",
  "parent_id": 203314075040467,
  "group_id": "b9a55a46-a9da-485b-9d6c-b5dc4aa24465",
  "alternates": [],
  "created_at": "2026-07-31T09:47:25.153Z",
  "deleted_at": null,
  "sort_by_date": null,
  "tag_list": [],
  "updated_at": "2026-08-12T15:03:52.451Z",
  "published_at": "2026-08-12T15:03:52.436Z",
  "id": 203987538594685,
  "uuid": "738e7771-d5cd-4b3a-8a16-6ce0c9ac6d05",
  "is_folder": false,
  "content": {
    "_uid": "a193ac06-4f1e-cc32-f3f8-70c2503ed514",
    "body": [
      {
        "_uid": "f0509606-682b-3d79-4869-dae9feff480f",
        "content": {
          "type": "doc",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "A collaboration between the Aga Khan University, Institute for Educational Development, East Africa (AKU-IED, EA), the Aga Khan Foundation (AKF) Tanzania, Madrasa Zanzibar, and the National Advisory Committee, F4L is a catalyst for profound educational change across the region.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "At the heart of the project is Rahaleo Primary School, where the transformative power of the Diploma in Leadership Management and Administration (DEMA) programme is evident. Headteacher Imani Hemedi Kulaga, a proud DEMA graduate, shared how the school has embraced gender equity in the classroom and how his students are actively engaged in learning. \"Now, when boys dominate answering questions, the girls confidently assert, 'It's our turn!'\" he proudly shared, reflecting a shift in classroom dynamics and a broader cultural change.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Further demonstrating the project's reach, the Regional Administrative Secretary (RAS) Zuwena Omary spoke of F4L as a cornerstone of educational development in Lindi. \"The F4L project has been pivotal in creating lasting improvements, and we are committed to ensuring this momentum continues,\" she said.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "Mingoyo Secondary School showcased a thriving microforest initiative, where 792 students are engaged in a climate resilience project, nurturing 340 trees with an innovative irrigation system. This initiative, driven by headteacher Fadhila Denis Mahundu, blends environmental stewardship with education, offering students experiential learning and linking classroom lessons to real-world challenges.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "In Nachingwea Teachers College, the Makerspace hub offers an exciting glimpse into the future of education. Deputy Head of College Deogratius Ndunguru highlighted the project's ripple effect, \"Students are creating teaching materials, and educators from other schools are coming here to learn and collaborate,\" he explained, emphasizing the culture of innovation that F4L is cultivating.",
                  "type": "text"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "content": [
                {
                  "text": "As the project's leaders reflected during their final meeting, the collective achievements of the F4L initiative highlight its potential to continue transforming education across Lindi. From empowered students to united communities of educators, F4L is a model for sustainable, long-term academic change. The challenge ahead is to scale up these successes even further, by continuing to invest in leadership, equity, and environmental education. F4L is paving the way for a brighter, more sustainable future for education in Tanzania.",
                  "type": "text"
                },
                {
                  "type": "hard_break"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
